WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.840
Christopher Nolan's 2020 sci-fi action thriller&nbsp;
Tenet is absolutely packed with exciting plot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:10.800
twists and complex concepts, so you probably&nbsp;
didn't pick up on everything in your first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:15.920
or even fifth, viewing. Keep watching to discover&nbsp;
some of the small details you may have missed.

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:20.560
If you've watched Tenet, then you have a basic&nbsp;
understanding of time inversion. You know there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:24.960
are turnstiles that can reverse an object's&nbsp;
entropy, allowing that object to move backwards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:30.640
through time. But processing who is and isn't&nbsp;
inverted can be a tedious task. Lucky for us,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:35.040
the makers of Tenet laced the film with color&nbsp;
theory to help us figure everything out.

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:39.920
In the final battle, soldiers are given arm bands&nbsp;
that are either blue or red to indicate whether or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:44.720
not they're inverted. Red signifies an object&nbsp;
that's not inverted and moving with the usual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:49.520
flow of time, while blue signifies an object&nbsp;
that's inverted and moving against the flow of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.520 --> 00:00:54.240
time. These identifiers are actually scattered&nbsp;
throughout the film. When Sator is tormenting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:59.200
his wife Kat, she's sporting a bright red dress.&nbsp;
And when the Protagonist and Neil find the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:03.680
turnstiles at Freeport, the doors they walk&nbsp;
through are labeled with red and blue signs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:07.760
The most notable color-rich moment comes&nbsp;
when Sator interrogates the Protagonist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.760 --> 00:01:12.240
and shoots Kat with an inverted round. The&nbsp;
room is bathed in blue light on one side,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:15.920
while the other side of the glass is lit&nbsp;
with red. Paying attention to the reds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:19.520
and blues will make another viewing of&nbsp;
Tenet a completely different experience.

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:24.880
After the Protagonist wakes up from his pill that&nbsp;
put him in a coma, he's greeted by an official&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:29.440
who informs him of the existence of Tenet, an&nbsp;
organization fighting an onslaught from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:34.240
future. The Protagonist is informed that using the&nbsp;
word will launch him further down the rabbit hole.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:38.560
You might think that the word is used this way&nbsp;
only once, when he meets with scientists to learn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:43.440
the mechanics of time inversion. But it's actually&nbsp;
dropped in conversation by other people in subtle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:48.560
ways. When the Protagonist executes the reverse&nbsp;
bungee jump with Neil, he then meets Priya,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.560 --> 00:01:53.520
the supplier of an inverted bullet found earlier,&nbsp;
and she tells him something very important.

00:01:53.520 --> 00:01:57.200
"To say anything about a client would&nbsp;
violate the tenets he lives by."

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:02.560
From Priya's perspective, she's already met&nbsp;
the Protagonist, though he hasn't met her yet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:06.640
This is a sneaky way for her to check which&nbsp;
version of the Protagonist she's talking to.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.640 --> 00:02:11.680
Her dialogue also tells us that she's viewing the&nbsp;
conceptual core with a "many worlds" mentality,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:15.920
which is incorrect. She alludes to this during&nbsp;
an ominous conversation when she states:

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:20.000
"If that universe can exist, we don't live in it."

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:23.680
Her inability to correctly process&nbsp;
cause and effect as non-linear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:25.520
results in a bullet from the Protagonist.

00:02:26.320 --> 00:02:30.400
The most hard-hitting scene in Tenet is&nbsp;
undoubtedly when we discover that the inverted man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:35.680
the Protagonist battled at Freeport is actually&nbsp;
himself. After Kat is hit with an inverted round,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:40.240
the crew invert themselves in order for the wound&nbsp;
to properly take its course without killing her.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.240 --> 00:02:43.840
In order to flip back around again,&nbsp;
they venture back to Freeport to jump&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:48.160
into the turnstiles that they found the week&nbsp;
before. Their plan is to sneak in during the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:52.640
panic after the plane crash from their earlier&nbsp;
heist. A hypnotic sequence then takes place in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:56.480
which they are running through the crash site&nbsp;
while the world moves in reverse around them.

00:02:56.480 --> 00:03:00.880
It's after this shot that the Protagonist&nbsp;
is thrust into his altercation with himself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.880 --> 00:03:05.120
The fight likely distracted you from some neat&nbsp;
little details, like the Protagonist's view of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:09.280
running out to tell Neil that it's all clear,&nbsp;
which cuts to Neil's perspective as he watches&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.280 --> 00:03:14.000
the Protagonist run by in reverse. The group&nbsp;
hijacks an ambulance and are able to escape the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:18.880
scene unharmed, for the most part. A clever detail&nbsp;
emerges as they are driving off in the ambulance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.880 --> 00:03:22.640
as you can see the inverted version of the&nbsp;
characters moving backwards away from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:27.280
plane crash with Kat on the gurney. This is just&nbsp;
one of several moments when Tenet's characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.280 --> 00:03:31.840
are completely unaware that they're passing&nbsp;
by different inverted versions of themselves.

00:03:32.480 --> 00:03:36.640
Tenet isn't a time travel movie in the typical&nbsp;
sense. Consider Neil and the Protagonist's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.640 --> 00:03:40.880
discussion of the "grandfather paradox," which&nbsp;
posits that if you were to go back in time and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:45.920
kill your own grandfather, you would cease to&nbsp;
exist, thereby resulting in a logical loop.

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:46.960
"What's the answer?"

00:03:46.960 --> 00:03:48.480
"There's no answer. It's a paradox."

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:52.960
Supposedly, the people of the future believe&nbsp;
that if they harm their own grandfather,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:56.880
there won't be consequences. Or at least,&nbsp;
none more severe than the planetary doom&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:00.000
they're already marching towards. If&nbsp;
their line of thinking is correct,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:03.360
then the fact that everything is taking&nbsp;
place means that they failed. Had they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:07.520
succeeded in triggering the algorithm, then&nbsp;
time wouldn't be flowing in our direction.

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:12.000
This could also be true according to some of the&nbsp;
film's other events, like Kat killing Sator in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:16.000
the past. This would mean that the past can be&nbsp;
affected without consequence, because there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.000 --> 00:04:20.800
a version of Sator running amok long after&nbsp;
his demise. This is actually left ambiguous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:25.120
as some of the dialogue tells us that there is&nbsp;
the possibility that this is a future version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:29.840
of Sator. This would mean that Sator and Kat&nbsp;
both inverted and came back to the same moment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.840 --> 00:04:34.160
So in reality, we still don't know which&nbsp;
take on the grandfather paradox is true&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.160 --> 00:04:38.320
within the world of Tenet. That feels about&nbsp;
right for a Christopher Nolan mind-bender.

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:43.680
An early step on the Protagonist's path to getting&nbsp;
close to Sator is their sailboat adventure.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:47.760
Kat is also on board, and she takes the&nbsp;
opportunity to unhook Sator's harness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:52.720
and send him tumbling into the water, nearly&nbsp;
killing him. The Protagonist's rescue helps&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:57.600
him move closer within Sator's circle, but this&nbsp;
scene has even more meaning to it than that.

00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:00.880
Different parts of this sequence are&nbsp;
presented as a visual metaphor for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.880 --> 00:05:04.880
movie's time mechanics. Consider the&nbsp;
color of the sailboat, for example:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:09.200
it's blue. It's so blue, in fact, that&nbsp;
it feels intentional. With this in mind,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:12.720
it's worth noting the shot of the sailboat&nbsp;
turning around against the current.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:17.680
This is a nod to how time inversion works: it&nbsp;
moves upstream, against the usual flow of time.

00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:22.640
After the Protagonist is told about Tenet,&nbsp;
he's dropped off at sea among a scattering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.640 --> 00:05:27.120
of windmills. You probably assumed at this point&nbsp;
that he's waiting for another boat to take him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:31.600
further to his next destination. While he's&nbsp;
waiting, he climbs a ladder inside the windmill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:36.240
and does some pull-ups. During a first watch,&nbsp;
this routine probably seems to exist only to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.240 --> 00:05:40.880
display his level of discipline. But this scene&nbsp;
comes back into play in a brief moment later on.

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:45.280
In that subsequent scene, the Protagonist is&nbsp;
inverted, and he travels back to the day an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:49.760
explosion occurs in Stalsk-12, which happens&nbsp;
at the same time as the opera siege in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.760 --> 00:05:54.160
opening scene. While working his way back,&nbsp;
there's a shot of him on a big yellow barge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.160 --> 00:05:58.640
doing pull-ups as the ship moves in reverse.&nbsp;
This area of the ocean is actually the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:03.200
windmill-filled area seen earlier. Thus, this&nbsp;
scene shows the Protagonist moving by his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:07.440
past self. Also, when his earlier version&nbsp;
leaves the windmill, it's on a blue boat,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:12.000
while all other boats on the water are red. In&nbsp;
the later scene on the yellow barge, you can see a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.000 --> 00:06:17.120
blue boat moving backwards next to a couple of red&nbsp;
boats. Remember: the entire movie is a palindrome.

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:21.440
There are a slew of confusing moments all&nbsp;
throughout Tenet, and wrapping your brain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.440 --> 00:06:25.520
around each one of them can cause a bit of a&nbsp;
headache. But one thing we're pretty sure we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.520 --> 00:06:29.920
say with certainty is that Neil is marching&nbsp;
towards his doom the last time we see him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.920 --> 00:06:34.880
While the Protagonist is down in the cavern trying&nbsp;
to procure the algorithm, he spots a piece of red&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:39.360
string on the backpack of the inverted soldier&nbsp;
who's lying dead on the other side of the locked&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:44.080
gate. As Neil is walking away, we see that&nbsp;
same piece of red string on his backpack.

00:06:44.080 --> 00:06:48.400
This isn't the first time in Tenet that we see&nbsp;
that key piece of string. In the opening scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:51.520
the Protagonist is a player in the&nbsp;
opera heist. There's a moment when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.520 --> 00:06:55.040
he's rescued by what appears to be&nbsp;
a man wielding an inverted bullet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.040 --> 00:06:59.760
As the man turns to run off, the Protagonist&nbsp;
spots that same piece of string on his backpack.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:03.760
This means that the Protagonist was being&nbsp;
saved by Neil before they ever actually met.

00:07:04.640 --> 00:07:07.920
Some Tenet theories make the assumption&nbsp;
that the pill the Protagonist takes at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:12.160
the beginning of the film successfully killed&nbsp;
him. They claim that we are witnessing his end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.160 --> 00:07:16.000
and that the movie is moving towards how it&nbsp;
all started. But the dialogue when he wakes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:19.760
up makes it clear that that is not the case,&nbsp;
although that pill does come back into play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.760 --> 00:07:25.280
later on. When Sator is contemplating his eventual&nbsp;
suicide, he holds up that same pill and reveals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.280 --> 00:07:29.840
that he borrowed it from the CIA. He goes on&nbsp;
to describe it as the way that the world ends:

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:32.640
"Not with a bang, but a whimper."

00:07:32.640 --> 00:07:36.080
This means that Sator is unaware of&nbsp;
the fact that the pill is a fake,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.080 --> 00:07:40.160
and it also means that if Kat doesn't kill&nbsp;
Sator, he will still fail to set off the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:45.280
algorithm by killing himself. This is basically&nbsp;
indicating that Sator was always going to lose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.280 --> 00:07:48.720
no matter what course of action he&nbsp;
takes. Whatever's happened, happened.

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:53.520
A common complaint about Christopher Nolan&nbsp;
films is their incredibly loud soundtracks.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:57.760
Sometimes they can overwhelm the dialogue,&nbsp;
and in a movie like Tenet, the dialogue is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.760 --> 00:08:02.240
extremely important. It's a situation that&nbsp;
can be easily remedied with subtitles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.240 --> 00:08:05.760
at least. We can then be content with the&nbsp;
sound levels, because you can't deny how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:09.840
effective the score is in setting the mood.&nbsp;
Moreover, there are several key moments in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.840 --> 00:08:13.840
which Tenet's music is particularly&nbsp;
creative, and dramatically important.

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:18.320
Composer Ludwig Göransson used a creative&nbsp;
approach to ensure that Tenet's score&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:22.480
mirrors the film's themes. At various&nbsp;
moments, such as the interrogation scene,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:27.520
the music is playing backwards. This detail&nbsp;
is so intricate and important that Göransson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:32.080
didn't just reverse the soundtrack; he actually&nbsp;
had his musicians play the music backwards.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.080 --> 00:08:35.840
It's not the only time he played with the&nbsp;
music this way. During the final battle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:40.400
there are two different scores playing over each&nbsp;
other so as to match a battlefield featuring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:45.760
inverted and non-inverted soldiers. Göransson went&nbsp;
even further in some scenes. When the Protagonist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.760 --> 00:08:50.720
is on a boat with both inverted and non-inverted&nbsp;
soldiers training behind him, the music has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.720 --> 00:08:54.800
its rhythm and percussion playing forward,&nbsp;
while the orchestra is playing in reverse.

00:08:55.600 --> 00:08:58.960
As Neil marches off to rejoin the&nbsp;
battle once again, he declares:

00:08:59.840 --> 00:09:01.920
"This whole operation's a temporal pincer."

00:09:02.640 --> 00:09:06.480
That statement helps us better understand&nbsp;
the movie as a whole, like how the ending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:11.440
occurs at the same time as the opening. During the&nbsp;
conclusion, the Protagonist is part of a battle in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:16.480
Stalsk-12, where everyone is fighting over the&nbsp;
algorithm. The soldiers we see are performing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.480 --> 00:09:21.200
"temporal pincer." In the traditional military&nbsp;
sense, a "pincer" involves attacking your enemy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.200 --> 00:09:25.440
from two different directions. If you take&nbsp;
that concept and correlate it with time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.440 --> 00:09:28.880
it means that the soldiers are attacking&nbsp;
from two different directions in time.

00:09:28.880 --> 00:09:33.120
The window of time that the soldiers work with&nbsp;
is ten minutes on either side of the event.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.120 --> 00:09:36.640
Everything that Christopher Nolan does&nbsp;
when making his films is intentional,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:41.680
and this chosen amount of time is no exception.&nbsp;
The word "Tenet" spells "ten" both forwards and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:45.920
backwards. Thus, the title of the movie&nbsp;
itself hints to what is going to happen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:50.560
Compared to the other details covered on this&nbsp;
list, this particular one is rather simple.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:53.680
But it's nevertheless dizzyingly&nbsp;
cool when you actually notice it.

